The test file uses `Verse(n)`, `Verses(start, stop)`, and `Song()`. Let me check the details:

- `Verse(n int) (string, error)` — returns a verse. Error for invalid verse (104). Verse 0 is valid ("No more bottles...").
- `Verses(start, stop int) (string, error)` — concatenates verses from start down to stop. Error cases: invalid start (109), invalid stop (-20), start less than stop (8, 14).
- `Song()` — returns the entire song, equal to Verses(99, 0).

Each verse ends with "\n", and verses are separated by a blank line. Looking at verses86: each verse is 2 lines + a trailing newline, and between verses there's a blank line. So the verse format is:

```
8 bottles of beer on the wall, 8 bottles of beer.\n
Take one down and pass it around, 7 bottles of beer on the wall.\n
\n
```

So when concatenating, each verse's text ends with "\n", followed by "\n" as a separator. In fact, verse8 = "8 bottles of beer on the wall, 8 bottles of beer.\nTake one down and pass it around, 7 bottles of beer on the wall.\n" — ends with a single newline. And verses86 ends with "5 bottles of beer on the wall.\n\n" — that is, verse3's text + "\n". So Verses joins verse texts with a separator of "\n" (which produces a blank line between them).

Verse 0: "No more bottles of beer on the wall, no more bottles of beer.\nGo to the store and buy some more, 99 bottles of beer on the wall.\n"

Verse 1: "1 bottle of beer on the wall, 1 bottle of beer.\nTake it down and pass it around, no more bottles of beer on the wall.\n"

So the bottle phrases:
- n > 1: "{n} bottles of beer"
- n == 1: "1 bottle of beer"
- n == 0: "no more bottles of beer"

Line 1: "{BottlePhrase(n)} on the wall, {lowercase first letter of BottlePhrase(n)}."
Line 2:
- n > 1: "Take one down and pass it around, {BottlePhrase(n-1)} on the wall."
- n == 1: "Take it down and pass it around, no more bottles of beer on the wall."
- n == 0: "Go to the store and buy some more, 99 bottles of beer on the wall."

